    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "heading": "mera / meri / mere — the gender of \"my\"",
      "body": "\"My\" changes to match the thing you own, not you:\n\n• ਮੇਰਾ (mera) — masculine singular: mera bhra, mera baapu\n• ਮੇਰੀ (meri) — feminine: meri maa, meri bhain, meri dadi\n• ਮੇਰੇ (mere) — masculine plural / oblique: mere bachche, mere do bhra han\n\nPunjabi nouns each carry a gender. Family words make a good first set to feel the pattern: bhra, baapu, dada, parivaar are masculine; maa, bhain, dadi are feminine."
    },
    {
      "type": "whyThisWay",
      "heading": "Why is it said this way?",
      "body": "English has one word — \"uncle\" — for many relationships. Punjabi splits them: ਚਾਚਾ (father's brother) and ਮਾਮਾ (mother's brother) are different people in the family map, and different words. The same applies to grandparents: dada/dadi are the father's parents, nana/nani the mother's.\n\nWhy? Joint-family life historically kept paternal relatives in the same household, so language grew labels that make the exact relationship unambiguous. When you learn the words, you're learning the family structure too."
    },
